    "3.4 Write property test for write/read round-trip preservation": {
      "status": "failed",
      "failingExample": "Falsifying example: test_write_read_round_trip_preserves_snapshot(\n    records=[{'measurement': 'itemPrice',\n              'tags': {'itemID': '554'},\n              'time': 1600000000,\n              'fields': {'avgHighPrice': 0, 'avgLowPrice': 0,\n                         'highPriceVolume': 0, 'lowPriceVolume': 0}}],\n)\n\nThe write succeeds against the live InfluxDB 3 Core container, but the read-back\nvia influx.query_price_series(...) fails with:\n\n    TypeError: Object of type datetime is not JSON serializable\n    (json/encoder.py, raised while the influxdb3-python Flight client serializes\n     query_parameters)\n\nRoot cause (in code, not the test): ge_pipeline.influx.query_price_series binds\nthe start/stop bounds as query_parameters by passing datetime objects\n(via _to_datetime). The influxdb3-python client serializes query_parameters as\nJSON before sending them over Flight+gRPC, and datetime is not JSON-serializable,\nso every parameterized time-range query raises before reaching the server.\n\nThis is a bug in the Query_Path time-parameter binding surfaced only against a\nreal server (mocked unit tests never serialize the params). Likely fix (future\ntask, not part of 3.4): bind time bounds as JSON-serializable values (e.g. unix\nseconds / epoch integers or RFC3339 strings) and compare with to_timestamp(...)\nin SQL, rather than binding raw datetime objects.",
      "lastRunTimestamp": 1788535189843
    },
    "3.6 Write property tests for latest-timestamp and distinct listing": {
      "status": "failed",
      "failingExample": "Property 6 (Latest timestamp equals the maximum stored) FAILED against a live InfluxDB 3 Core container.\n\nFalsifying example: test_get_latest_timestamp_equals_maximum_stored(timestamps=[1599999900])\n  assert latest == max(timestamps)\n  assert 1 == 1599999900\n\nRoot cause (implementation bug, not a test bug): the write path stamps timestamps with the wrong precision. `_record_to_point` builds `Point(...).time(record[\"time\"])`, and `Point.time()` defaults to `write_precision='ns'`. `write_batch` calls `client.write(record=[...], write_precision=\"s\")`, but the per-point ns precision wins, so a unix-seconds value like 1599999900 is stored as 1599999900 NANOSECONDS -> 1970-01-01 00:00:01.599999900 (verified directly against the container: stored time = Timestamp('1970-01-01 00:00:01.599999900'), get_latest_timestamp returns 1).\n\nThis same bug also fails Property 5 (write/read round-trip, task 3.4). Property 10 (Distinct item listing) PASSED. Fix belongs in ge_pipeline/influx.py (pass seconds precision into Point.time or convert seconds->ns), which is task 3.3/3.5 implementation work, not the property test.",
      "lastRunTimestamp": 1788535338685
    },
